#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292711 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f87bc14d-5e68-5a5e-bec8-f1b5b21c02e8" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Arc Length of a Smooth Curve Using the Arc Length Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2cfdb7c3-fb14-545a-84b4-cc17cf2352b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292713} {:answer/id #uuid "2009f87b-8f15-570f-9bda-1b41c9aee810" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292714} {:answer/id #uuid "aabfc78e-66e6-5edf-820f-dd3ddd4bbfe6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292715} {:answer/id #uuid "3de33f0b-6fbd-5284-a6a7-464996ab991e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292716} {:answer/id #uuid "ef101227-9347-5b0f-887a-58c6b7c4a2d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292717}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aabfc78e-66e6-5edf-820f-dd3ddd4bbfe6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292715} :answer-field/id #uuid "16ea3d48-f723-5f62-8215-c2767ccc9abc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292712}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "68cf6bb9-4815-5cbd-af07-6f1b5adb668a" :question/math-academy-id "q-122287" :question/problem "Compute the arc length of $y=\\frac{2}{3}{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3/2}$ for $x\\in [0,3].$" :question/worked-solution "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\frac{2}{3}{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3/2},$ $a=0,$ and $b=3.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{(1+{x}^{2})}^{1/2}\\cdot 2x=2x\\sqrt{(1+{x}^{2})}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1+{[2x\\sqrt{(1+{x}^{2})}]}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2{x}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}=2{x}^{2}+1$ to the formula for the arc length, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(2{x}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}+x)|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =[\\frac{2}{3}(3)^{3}+(3)]-[\\frac{2}{3}(0)^{3}+(0)] \\\\  & =21.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384384 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "55f783c4-25d6-5855-877e-31bed16b12c4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Possible Curve Given Its Arc Length As an Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04ca7e2d-bb8a-5a50-938f-13f30519ca7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2{e}^{2x}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384386} {:answer/id #uuid "e1e3190a-8a8f-5d8c-8d8d-3722e30327be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{x}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384387} {:answer/id #uuid "3e98169d-ad38-5e1c-a020-a3019ff85b4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3{e}^{3x}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384388} {:answer/id #uuid "e52495e9-0eec-5317-ab7f-855e4da6c39b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2{e}^{3x}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384389} {:answer/id #uuid "86dcabcf-02c2-5f15-9242-bbe76a3b04ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{3x}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384390}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e52495e9-0eec-5317-ab7f-855e4da6c39b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2{e}^{3x}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384389} :answer-field/id #uuid "70604b24-ad6b-573f-a84f-c0427a6d9cec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384385}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "26e11feb-576b-5f89-bcb1-c0e37dc1ab2e" :question/math-academy-id "q-122365" :question/problem "The length of a curve $y=f(x)$ is given by ${\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{1+36{e}^{6x}}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Which of the following could be  an equation for this curve?" :question/worked-solution "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that \n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{1+36{e}^{6x}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComparing the above with the arc length formula, we see that we must have\n\n$$\n[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}=36{e}^{6x}\\,⟹\\,{f}^{′}(x)=\\pm 6{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating the above equation with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\pm 6\\int {e}^{3x}\\text{ d}x \\\\  & =\\pm 2{e}^{3x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, the only one that has the required form is $y=2{e}^{3x}+1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384391 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f87bc14d-5e68-5a5e-bec8-f1b5b21c02e8" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Arc Length of a Smooth Curve Using the Arc Length Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "de481db3-c405-5a31-8a80-acd98c2f1a9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384393} {:answer/id #uuid "b00cecac-8428-564d-997a-c8a9dcd92e8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{29}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384394} {:answer/id #uuid "bb4a88bf-7563-502c-8b76-4b411f1d217e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{4}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384395} {:answer/id #uuid "07c02ea3-54d2-53c4-97d1-e71d7eebc272" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡4+\\frac{15}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384396} {:answer/id #uuid "640f9827-b5a6-5261-8dde-58b6a8cba530" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384397}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07c02ea3-54d2-53c4-97d1-e71d7eebc272" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡4+\\frac{15}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384396} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e388846-ab83-514e-8fda-927ace8262c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384392}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "430a9509-653c-5419-a6b3-7926dc6bcc6a" :question/math-academy-id "q-5998" :question/problem "Compute the arc length of $y=\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ for $x\\in [1,4].$" :question/worked-solution "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ $a=1,$ and $b=4.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{4}x-\\frac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{1}{4}x-\\frac{1}{x})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{16}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{16}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+\\frac{16}{{x}^{2}}+8} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{{x}^{2}+4}{x})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+4}{4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}=\\frac{{x}^{2}+4}{4x}$ into the arc length formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{{x}^{2}+4}{4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{x}{4}+\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{2})|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)+\\frac{1}{8}(4)^{2}]-[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)+\\frac{1}{8}(1)^{2}] \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡4+2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\frac{1}{8} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡4+\\frac{15}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337465 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "55f783c4-25d6-5855-877e-31bed16b12c4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Possible Curve Given Its Arc Length As an Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18433ded-ba7e-52ee-8921-d06888eea8bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337467} {:answer/id #uuid "2e6b9962-a78d-520c-a5f1-4696af0045af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{(1-x)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337468} {:answer/id #uuid "d80f015c-9f57-57ff-b3b3-76968703a7cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{1+x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337469} {:answer/id #uuid "e373f9c7-6b4f-56c1-aa9c-8d907c144ec4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{1-x}+\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337470} {:answer/id #uuid "5459e367-7b1c-5808-9d42-ba9227ea2a55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-x)+7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337471}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5459e367-7b1c-5808-9d42-ba9227ea2a55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-x)+7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337471} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9f9ce59-0774-5034-a0a7-9893cebb905f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337466}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8d968cc2-d41a-56be-9db0-c9b59a5e633c" :question/math-academy-id "q-82619" :question/problem "The length of a curve $y=f(x)$ is given by ${\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{(1-x)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Which of the following could be  an equation for this curve?" :question/worked-solution "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe're given that \n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{(1-x)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComparing the above with the arc length formula, we see that we must have\n\n$$\n[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}=\\frac{1}{(1-x)^{2}}\\,⟹\\,{f}^{′}(x)=\\pm \\frac{1}{1-x}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating the above equation with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\pm \\int \\frac{1}{1-x}\\text{ d}x \\\\  & =\\pm (-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1-x|)+C \\\\  & =\\pm \\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-x)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, the only one that has the required form is $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-x)+7.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384398 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f87bc14d-5e68-5a5e-bec8-f1b5b21c02e8" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Arc Length of a Smooth Curve Using the Arc Length Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c96caac-0266-5fb5-9089-3708269cad91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{343}{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384400} {:answer/id #uuid "06aea639-0d69-5dd2-91c0-20f0d0bab617" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{57}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384401} {:answer/id #uuid "ec7e5507-7766-5f22-bfe4-a480513d105a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384402} {:answer/id #uuid "4ceff9f8-70f8-584b-a1ce-f8e2281bce83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{57}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384403} {:answer/id #uuid "2f7c54dc-dec5-5bb8-8469-b26a65256ad9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{43}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384404}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ceff9f8-70f8-584b-a1ce-f8e2281bce83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{57}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384403} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7926d01-104d-5fcb-94ee-6efd88d03b45" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384399}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e450bbc2-61d4-5e3c-b27d-429a6e98ef00" :question/math-academy-id "q-85596" :question/problem "Compute the arc length of $y=\\frac{8}{3}{x}^{3/2}$ for $x\\in [0,3].$" :question/worked-solution "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between two points where $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula:\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\frac{8}{3}{x}^{3/2},$ $a=0,$ and $b=3.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=4\\sqrt{x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${f}^{′}(x)=4\\sqrt{x}$ to the formula for the arc length, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{1+[4\\sqrt{x}]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{1+16x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate this integral using the substitution $u=1+16x.$ Then, $\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{16}\\text{d}u.$\n\nWe also need to compute new limits:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & 0 & 3 \\\\ u & 1 & 49\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{1}^{49}\\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{16}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}{\\int }_{1}^{49}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}{[\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}]|}_{1}^{49} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}({49}^{3/2}-{1}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{343-1}{24} \\\\  & =\\frac{57}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
